abstract:
We present a highly stable and miniaturised optical system designed for a caesium satellite-borne cold atom clock (CSCAC). The optical system was integrated on a <inline-formula xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><tex-math notation="LaTeX">$224\,{\mathop{m mm}
olimits} 	imes 195\,{\mathop{m mm}
olimits} 	imes 112\,{\mathop{m mm}
olimits} $</tex-math></inline-formula> aluminium-based silicon carbide bench and emitted laser beams with four different frequencies on three fiber ports to capture, cool, prepare, and detect atoms. The optical system, utilizing a compact three-layer optical-mechanical and thermal management design, passed environmental simulation tests and adapted to a special satellite-borne environment. The optical system stability meets the laser requirements of the clock.
